meta {
  name: Ready
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{student_url}}/ready
  body: none
  auth: none
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.status: eq ready
}

tests {
  test("reports an environment name", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().env, "env").to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  Readiness, the counterpart to /health: this one DOES query the database, so a
  200 here proves the server can actually serve rather than merely that the
  process is up.

  This is the only place that round trip is genuinely exercised. The in-process
  suite stubs the check, because it runs on in-memory SQLite while the probe
  uses the application engine — so a real `SELECT 1` against a real database
  only happens here.

  If this fails while /health passes, the API is running but its database is
  unreachable: check the connection string and that Postgres is up, rather than
  looking for an application bug.
}
